For all $
u \in 1, 2, \ldots, n$, let $\closedint {x_{
u - 1} } {x_
u}$ be a closed subinterval of $\closedint a b$.
As $f$ is bounded on $\closedint a b$, it is bounded on $\closedint {x_{
u - 1} } {x_
u}$.
So, let $m_
u$ be the infimum and $M_
u$ be the supremum of $\map f x$ on the interval $\closedint {x_{
u - 1} } {x_
u}$.
By definition, $m_
u \le M_
u$.
So $m_{
u} \paren {x_
u - x_{
u - 1} } \le M_{
u} \paren {x_
u - x_{
u - 1} }$.
It follows directly that $\displaystyle \sum_{
u \mathop = 1}^n m_
u \paren {x_
u - x_{
u - 1} } \le \sum_{
u \mathop = 1}^n M_
u \paren {x_
u - x_{
u - 1} }$.
